    "rawText": "28 \nWhy CO₂ is especially important in backend \nUPW has extremely high resistivity:\nUPW ≈ 18.2 MΩ·cm → extremely poor electrical conductivity\nDuring wafer dicing, grinding, high-pressure spraying, or spin rinsing, flowing UPW \ncan generate electrostatic charge. This can cause electrostatic discharge (ESD) damage\nto sensitive devices.\nAdding a controlled amount of CO₂:\nCO₂ + H₂O ⇌H₂CO₃ ⇌H⁺ + HCO₃⁻\nincreases ionic conductivity and therefore reduces water resistivity, allowing \naccumulated charge to dissipate.\nPure UPW → high resistivity → static charge risk\nCO₂ + UPW → higher conductivity → lower static charge → ESD protection\nThis is why carbonated DI water / CO₂-DIW is particularly common around wafer dicing \nand some backend wet processes.\n \n",
