    "rawText": "94 \nFundamental Principles of Plasma Etching \nA material can be etched efficiently only if the surface reaction produces a by-product that can \nleave the wafer surface under the process conditions.\nEtchant species + wafer material → volatile reaction product → desorption → vacuum pump\nIf the product is nonvolatile, it stays on the surface, causing residue, micromasking, roughness, \netch stop, or requiring stronger ion bombardment / higher temperature.\n1. Silicon etched with ﬂuorine: Si(s) + 4F• → SiF₄(g)\nSiF₄ is highly volatile, so it readily leaves the wafer.\nTherefore: F radical → reacts with Si → SiF₄ gas → pumped away\nThis is why fluorine chemistry can produce a very high Si etch rate.\n2. If Material + Etchant → nonvolatile solid\nthe product remains on the wafer:\nReaction → surface residue → blocks fresh etchant → etch slows/stops\nThus, selecting F, Cl, or Br is partly about asking:\nWhich halogen forms the most removable product with the material I want to etch?\n \n",
