    "rawText": "96 \nThe Complete Selection Logic for Etching Gas \nWhy It Matters\nQuestion\nReaction must be chemically possible\n1. Will F, Cl or Br react with the material?\nSiF₄? AlCl₃? WF₆? InClₓ?\n2. What product will form?\nDetermines whether it can leave the wafer\n3. Is the product volatile?\nDetermines wafer/chamber temperature\n4. At what temperature is it removable?\nDetermines bias/RIE/ICP requirement\n5. Is ion bombardment required?\nDetermines selectivity\n6. What is the mask reaction?\nDetermines anisotropy\n7. What happens on the sidewall?\nDetermines particles/roughness\n8. Are nonvolatile residues formed?\nDetermines abatement\n9. What are the exhaust products?\nDetermines final recipe\n10. Does the chemistry meet CD/profile requirements?\nIf the etch product is volatile → it leaves → etching continues.\nIf the etch product is nonvolatile → it stays → etching slows/stops, unless ions or heat remove it.\nThis concept explains why SF₆ is excellent for fast Si etching, HBr is excellent for precise Si \netching, Cl₂/BCl₃ is favored for many metals and III-Vs, and C₄F₈ deliberately creates nonvolatile \npolymer for sidewall protection.\n \n",
