    "rawText": "99 \nRole of C/F ratio in fluorocarbon plasma \netching \nC/F ratio is one of the most useful ways to understand the balance between chemical etching \nand fluorocarbon-polymer deposition/passivation.\nMore F → stronger chemical etching. \nMore C relative to F → more CFₓ polymer → stronger passivation and anisotropy\nTypical result / use\nEtch tendency\nPolymer \nformation\nPlasma \nbehavior\nC/F \ncondition\nHigh etch rate, less sidewall \nprotection\nStrong chemical etching\nVery low\nF-rich\nVery low \nC/F\nSi etching, oxide etching with \nlower polymer\nEtching dominates\nLow\nF-rich\nLow C/F\nAnisotropic dielectric etching\nEtch + passivation\nMedium\nBalanced F + \nCFₓ\nMedium \nC/F\nHigh-selectivity oxide etching, \nsidewall protection\nPolymer/passivation \nincreases\nHigh\nC-rich\nHigh C/F\nEtch stop, chamber polymer \nbuildup possible\nDeposition may compete \nwith etching\nVery high\nStrongly \npolymerizing\nVery high \nC/F\nActual plasma chemistry also depends strongly on RF power/bias, pressure, source power, gas dilution, \nO₂ addition, residence time, chamber wall condition, and ion energy.\n \n",
