    "rawText": "Chemical Amplified Resist (CARs) \n25 \nCARs use a catalytic cycle to achieve high sensitivity in semiconductor lithography. During exposure, \nlight activates a Photoacid Generator (PAG) to release an acid. During a PEB, this acid acts as a catalyst \nto cleave protecting groups on thousands of polymer chains, causing a dramatic change in solubility.\nExposure                           \n(Acid Generation)\nDUV directly activates PAG, while \nEUV generates secondary electrons \nthat trigger photoacid formation.\nPost-Exposure Bake (PEB) \n(Catalytic Deprotection)\nPEB drives acid diffusion and \ndeprotection, making exposed \nresist soluble in TMAH.\nQuenching                       \n(Precision Control)\nBase quenchers limit acid \ndiffusion, reducing acid blur \nand improving resolution.\nDevelopment \nDevelopment\nremoves\nexposed positive-tone CAR or\nretains cross-linked negative-\ntone CAR\nCoating and \nPrebake\nExposure\nCoating and \nPrebake\nExposure\nDevelop\nPEB\nPR\nPAG\nPR\nPAG\nPhotoacid\nPhotoacid\nPositive \nCAR\nNegative \nCAR\nDevelop\nPEB\nDeprotection \nreaction\nCrosslinking \nreaction\n",
