    "rawText": "Thick Photoresist (5- 200 µmT)\nFor HAR patterns with excellent pattern fidelity, mechanical strength and chemical resistance \nThick photoresist is the same material as \nconventional photoresist but applied in \nmuch greater thickness to form robust \nhigh-aspect-ratio (HAR) structures\nIt provides excellent pattern fidelity, \nmechanical strength and chemical \nresistance during subsequent process\n(1) What is thick photoresist?\n(2) Key Benefits\nHigh-aspect-ratio (HAR) \npatterning\nEnables deep and vertical \nstructures\nPlanarization Capability\nHelps fill topography and \nachieve uniform surfaces\nProcess Robustness\nOffers good mechanical \nstrength and handling \nstability\nExcellent Etch Resistance\nWithstands aggressive \netching during process \nsteps\n(3) Applications\n(4) Typical Thickness Range\n(5) Common suppliers\nUsed as an etch mask for \ndeep trenches, TSVs, and \nMEMS structures\nDefines thick mold \ncavities and redistribution \nlayer openings\nFabricates HAR structures \nsuch as beams, comb \ndrives, and micro-channels\nEnables isolation trenches \nand vertical structures in \npower semiconductors\n(IGBT, MOSFET)\nUsed for defining \nwaveguides, grating, and \nother photonic structures\n•\nPositive-tone thick PR\n(AZ-P4620, SPR220, Microresist Technology, etc.)\n•\nNegative-tone thick PR\n(SU-8, SLAR, etc.)\n•\nDry film thick PR\nDeep Silicon Etching \n(DRIE)\nWafer Level Packaging\n(WLP)\nMEMS\nPower Devices\nPhotonic Devices\n•\nThickness depends on application requirements\n•\nHigher thickness higher aspect ratio capability\n35 \n",
