    "rawText": "21 \nCMP Slurry: Particle Size and Zeta Potential\nZeta potential is the electrokinetic potential at the slipping plane of particles suspended in a \nliquid. It influences electrostatic repulsion, colloidal stability, and particle–wafer interactions.\nGeneral Dispersion Behavior\nZeta Potential\nOften good electrostatic stability\nζ ≤ −30 mV or ≥ +30 mV\nModerate or formulation-\ndependent stability\n−30 to −10 mV or +10 to +30 \nmV\nHigher aggregation risk without \nother stabilization\n−10 to +10 mV\nImpact on CMP Performance\n•\nRemoval Rate (RR): Particle size, concentration, and surface charge affect mechanical and \nchemical interactions.\n•\nDefectivity: Agglomerated particles and large-particle tails increase scratching risk.\n•\nSlurry Stability: Sufficient electrostatic or steric repulsion helps prevent particle aggregation.\n•\nPost-CMP Cleaning: Particle–wafer electrostatic interactions influence particle adhesion and \nremoval.\n",
